ARTICLE Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) has had a solid 2026, rising about 21% so far this year. While that's beating the market, Nvidia investors are used to far greater returns. Fortunately for its shareholders, I think October could be the month that flips it around.
The market still isn't pricing any of Nvidia's massive expected growth in 2027 into the shares, but it could start to take that into account as the AI hyperscalers roll out their 2027 guidance for data center capital expenditures. That could confirm everything Nvidia has said about its expectations.
If this happens, Nvidia stock is primed to soar, as it's currently priced at a pretty attractive level.
Q3 results could reveal some incredible expectations
Nvidia has gotten a bit ahead of the curve in issuing guidance. During Q2, it revealed an incredible outlook that the market quite frankly shrugged off. The company announced that it expects to deliver 70% revenue growth during fiscal 2028 (which will end in January 2028). Considering Nvidia's size as a $5 trillion company, that growth rate is simply incredible.
Sales to AI hyperscalers are driving that forecast, as this cohort is expected to ramp up its spending on data centers again in 2027. The big five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on data center build-outs this year, and the current estimates are for their capex to rise to $1.3 trillion next year. That's just according to Nvidia's projections, which are likely pretty accurate, given what it knows about its key customers' plans.
These AI hyperscalers likely have their 2027 expansion plans set in stone already and have provided demand forecasts to Nvidia so the company can properly prepare its supply chain to meet them as far as is possible. So, I think investors can trust these projections, but the market really hasn't put any weight on them.
Given how large some of the build-out plans are expected to be, don't be surprised to see some of the AI hyperscalers start to reveal their 2027 capital expenditure guidance during Q3 earnings, as it will give the market more time to digest these massive figures. Many companies report in the second half of October, which makes the start of the month a great time to load up on Nvidia shares, as the market apparently isn't quite convinced that Nvidia will see as much growth as it's projecting.
However, this trailing earnings ratio doesn't reflect any of next year's estimated 70% growth, nor the growth Nvidia has delivered over the last two quarters of this fiscal year. Those results will make the stock look incredibly cheap, and if we value Nvidia using fiscal 2028 earnings projections, the stock tumbles to an incredibly cheap ratio of 14.
I think a fair, long-term valuation for this company would be about 30 times earnings. If Nvidia meets all analysts' expectations and rises to 30 times trailing earnings, the stock could more than double between now and the end of its fiscal 2028. That makes Nvidia a stock well worth buying today .
Its rally could kick off during October, making now the ideal time to buy, as the market may start to take Nvidia's long-term projections a bit more seriously once the hyperscalers start to roll out guidance for next year.

 12 TOPIC Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them. DESC Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027? QUESTION {'object': 'chip-export-controls', 'kind': 'theme', 'focus': "Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.", 'question': "By the end of NVIDIA's FY2027, will U.S. export authorization cover sales of current Blackwell-generation AI chips to approved customers in China, and will Chinese authorities no longer restrict those shipments, as established by official U.S. export-control or licensing decisions and Chinese regulatory or customs actions?", 'year': '2027', 'due_day': '2027-01-31', 'rungs': [{'label': 'Yes', 'condition': "By 2027-01-31, U.S. export authorization covers sales of NVIDIA's current Blackwell-generation AI chips to approved customers in China, and Chinese authorities no longer restrict those shipments."}, {'label': 'Ruled out', 'condition': 'After 2026-09-23, one of these authorities formally prohibits the relevant Blackwell shipments through 2027-01-31, preventing the required U.S. authorization and Chinese acceptance from both being in place by that date. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-23 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not.'}, {'label': 'Still open', 'condition': 'Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.'}]} OTHER {'not_judged': [], 'reference': None, 'favourable_end': None, 'favourable_why': None, 'ruled_out_by': 'The U.S. Department of Commerce or Bureau of Industry and Security, or the Chinese authorities responsible for regulating or clearing Nvidia chip shipments.', 'due_day_basis': 'period_end'}
ARTICLE Will Nvidia be allowed to sell more chips to China? This former Trump economic adviser has a hot take.
When Nvidia ( NVDA ) CEO Jensen Huang arrives at the state dinner on Thursday between President Trump and Chinese President Xi Jinping, you better believe he'll be hoping to get a sense of when — and to what extent — his business may resume in China.
Both sides coming to a deal on this front won't be easy, given the politics still at play.
"My personal opinion is that we need to have as much competitive advantage here in the United States as we can, because we've got competitive disadvantages against China," said Gary Cohn, IBM ( IBM ) vice chair and former economic adviser to President Trump in his first term, on Yahoo Finance's Sozzi Unleashed . "You know, they don't have environmental issues. They don't have a cost of capital. They can build data centers wherever they want. The citizens can't stand up and say not in my backyard."
Cohn added, "To the extent that the Chinese have all these other significant advantages, the one advantage we have is we've got the chips, and we've got the chip manufacturing here. I'd like to keep that advantage as long as we can. "
The Trump administration has allowed Nvidia to ship its H200 AI chips to approved customers in China. The problem for Nvidia: The H200 is already an older-generation product, trailing Nvidia's current Blackwell chips in terms of performance.
In other words, Nvidia is likely leaving billions in sales on the table by being unable to get Blackwell chips into China to feed the country's AI startup scene.
Chinese authorities, however, have so far restricted shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips.
While Nvidia hasn't factored any sales from China into its outlook for the year, the stock is still up 21% year to date, powered by the AI boom here in the US.

 21 TOPIC The big five hyperscalers' combined capex for calendar 2026 and calendar 2027, versus the approximately $800 billion for 2026 and roughly $1.3 trillion for 2027 the article reports they plan to spend. DESC How much combined capital expenditure will the five largest hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft, Meta Platforms and Oracle — spend in calendar 2026 and calendar 2027? QUESTION {'no_ladder': 'The tracked capex figure spans calendar 2026 and 2027 rather than one calendar year, and neither the topic nor the item gives its publication day.'} OTHER {'not_judged': None, 'reference': None, 'favourable_end': None, 'favourable_why': None, 'ruled_out_by': None, 'due_day_basis': None}
ARTICLE Artificial intelligence (AI) has created some enormous multibaggers for investors, but none are quite on the same level as Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) . When ChatGPT helped ignite the generative AI boom in late 2022, Nvidia was worth less than $400 billion. Today, the chipmaker's market capitalization has ballooned to roughly $5.6 trillion -- making it the most valuable company in the world .
This parabolic ascent shows how Nvidia evolved from a generic semiconductor company known for gaming GPUs into the undisputed king of AI infrastructure. Let's assess what might happen if you invested $500 in Nvidia every month from now through the end of 2030. Spoiler alert: Big gains could be in store.
How big can Nvidia get?
Before crunching any numbers, I need to come up with a baseline of where Nvidia might be headed over the next several years. The company generated $96.2 billion in revenue during its most recent quarter, representing a staggering 106% increase from the prior year. Even more impressive, Nvidia's data center business generated $89 billion, growing 117% year over year.
The next chapter is already quietly unfolding. Nvidia's Vera Rubin architecture is moving into full production. The company says the platform can deliver 10x the agentic AI throughput of its previous generation Grace Blackwell architecture. This matters because AI spending is moving beyond simply training large language models (LLMs). Reasoning models, AI agents , robotics, and other inference workloads require enormous amounts of continuous computing power.
Meanwhile, infrastructure commitments from big tech are getting larger. The big five hyperscalers -- Amazon , Alphabet , Microsoft , Meta Platforms , and Oracle -- plan to spend approximately $800 billion in capital expenditures (capex) in 2026 alone and roughly $1.3 trillion in 2027. Meanwhile, Anthropic has reportedly committed at least $518 billion to AI infrastructure over the next decade.
Nvidia is also expanding how it can monetize data centers. The company isn't merely selling GPUs anymore. AI factories now combine GPUs with other solutions from Nvidia, including CPUs, NVLink, networking, BlueField data processing units, storage infrastructure, and software platforms like CUDA .
I think complementing the legacy GPU business with adjacent solutions should help Nvidia expand its market share in the data center realm. Against this backdrop, I think the company can rise from a $5.6 trillion market value today to $15 trillion by 2030.
What investing $500 a month in Nvidia looks like
An investor putting $500 into Nvidia every month would invest $6,000 over the course of a full year. Assuming the strategy begins in October 2026 and continues through December 2030, the contributions would total $25,500.
Unfortunately, calculating the ending value isn't as simple as multiplying $25,500 by Nvidia's expected return. The reason is because you will be using a dollar-cost averaging strategy over the lifetime of the investment. This means the initial $500 invested in October 2026 has more than four years to appreciate, while a $500 investment made near the end of 2030 has almost no time to compound.
If I assume Nvidia's market capitalization rises gradually from $5.6 trillion to $15 trillion by the end of 2030, that works out to a 163% cumulative increase, or roughly 26% annualized or 1.9% per month when compounded. While this is a bit aggressive, I think it is also a reasonable upside scenario considering it's nowhere near the returns Nvidia generated during earlier stages of the AI boom.
If I run the calculated return across all 51 monthly investments, the $25,500 investment could finish 2030 worth approximately $43,400. That represents roughly $17,900 in investment gains, or 70% upside.
Obviously, no one knows what Nvidia will be worth four years from now. If the company reaches a $10 trillion valuation by the end of 2030, the same $500 per month strategy would grow to roughly $34,400. If the AI infrastructure supercycle sends Nvidia all the way to $20 trillion, the investment could approach $51,600 -- nearly a 100% gain.
The first takeaway here is that Nvidia has enough structural tailwinds to support meaningful share price appreciation over the next several years. The second takeaway is to understand is that my estimates assume Nvidia's share price generally follows a smooth, compounded path toward the 2030 target.
In reality, the stock's movement will almost certainly be messier. This means the price could rise or fall significantly from month to month. That's actually the entire point of dollar-cost averaging: rather than trying to time the market and predict exactly when Nvidia's stock will rise or fall, investors consistently put the same amount of money to work. For someone investing consistently every month, patience will ultimately matter just as much as finding the right opportunities.
By Adam Spatacco – Oct 6, 2026 at 12:00PM EST

 22 TOPIC Whether the proposed up-to-$125 billion hardware residual-value guarantee is signed and disclosed, and at what amount, versus remaining a proposal or being reduced or withdrawn. DESC Will Nvidia finalize its proposed sharing of up to $125 billion in risk by guaranteeing hardware residual values for its AI infrastructure financing platform by the check date? QUESTION {'object': 'NVDA', 'kind': 'asset', 'focus': 'Whether the proposed up-to-$125 billion hardware residual-value guarantee is signed and disclosed, and at what amount, versus remaining a proposal or being reduced or withdrawn.', 'question': 'Will Nvidia sign and disclose, in a definitive agreement or SEC filing, a finalized hardware residual-value guarantee for its AI infrastructure financing platform by April 3, 2028?', 'year': '2028', 'due_day': '2028-04-03', 'rungs': [{'label': 'Yes', 'condition': 'Nvidia signs a definitive agreement or files a 10-Q or 10-K disclosing the finalized amount and terms of its hardware residual-value guarantee for the AI infrastructure financing platform by April 3, 2028.'}, {'label': 'Ruled out', 'condition': 'After October 3, 2026, Nvidia formally withdraws the proposed guarantee or publicly decides not to provide any hardware residual-value guarantee for the platform. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-10-03 and stands on 2028-04-03, and only when rung 1 does not.'}, {'label': 'Still open', 'condition': 'Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-04-03.'}]} OTHER {'not_judged': [], 'reference': {'words': 'Nvidia also proposed sharing up to $125 billion in risk by guaranteeing hardware residual values.', 'kind': 'expected', 'value': '125000000000', 'value_to': None}, 'favourable_end': None, 'favourable_why': None, 'ruled_out_by': 'Nvidia', 'due_day_basis': 'stated'}
ARTICLE Amazon Pursues $8 Billion Sale-and-Leaseback of Nvidia Chips
Amazon is pursuing a sale-leaseback structure for approximately $8 billion worth of Nvidia AI chips, transferring them to outside investors while continuing to use them. The company plans to raise funds through bond issuance via a special purpose vehicle, with Amazon retaining use of the chips. The move is seen as a strategy to relieve AI investment burdens from its balance sheet amid capital expenditures that have ballooned to $220 billion this year. As the AI industry increasingly treats semiconductors as financial assets, the deal—if completed—could trigger similar asset securitization moves by other Big Tech companies.
Amazon is pursuing a sale-leaseback structure for approximately $8 billion worth of Nvidia artificial intelligence chips, transferring them to outside investors while continuing to lease and use them. The strategy aims to relieve the surging burden of AI infrastructure investment from its balance sheet while maintaining data center computing capacity.
According to the Financial Times, Amazon has recently been in contact with investors to discuss transferring approximately $8 billion worth of Nvidia "Grace Blackwell" chips deployed in U.S. data centers to a special purpose vehicle (SPV). The assets include thousands of AI chips installed across more than a dozen data centers in five U.S. states, including Nevada and Virginia.
The structure works as follows: Amazon transfers the chips it has secured to an SPV, which then issues bonds to raise capital from outside investors. Amazon subsequently leases the chips back and continues to use them in its existing data centers. This allows the company to reduce the burden of carrying expensive AI chips directly on its books while maintaining the computing capacity it needs.
Amazon is reportedly discussing offering investors up to 10% equity in the vehicle while retaining no equity stake itself. Bond investors are expecting investment-grade ratings based on Amazon's double-A credit rating rather than the residual value of the hardware. This is a key factor enabling participation from conservative institutional capital such as insurers and pension funds.
If the deal closes, Amazon would be able to offload approximately $8 billion in asset exposure while maintaining use of its AI chips. Big Tech faces a dilemma: it cannot halt investment in the AI race, but carrying the full cost on its balance sheet is burdensome—and that tension is now driving new financial engineering.
Astronomical Investment Costs Spur Financial Innovation
The backdrop to Amazon's resort to this financing method is the astronomical growth in AI investment costs. Amazon's projected capital expenditures for this year stand at approximately $220 billion, up about 10% from the previous forecast of $200 billion. The surge is driven by aggressive investment in data centers and AI infrastructure, centered on its cloud business, Amazon Web Services (AWS).
The primary driver of the upward revision is rising memory prices for AI servers. Demand continues to outstrip supply. AWS's backlog reached $496 billion, posting triple-digit year-over-year growth.
Andy Jassy, Amazon's CEO, said in July that even with significantly increased investment, the company would struggle to meet all AI computing demand.
At the time, he stated: "We won't have enough capacity to satisfy all the demand we have in 2026, and the same will be true in 2027." He added that "the demand already secured for 2028 is extraordinary." The sale-leaseback structure is seen as a compromise to address the demand pressure he referenced while preserving financial soundness.
Across the AI industry, there is a growing movement to treat semiconductors as a form of financial asset. Companies are borrowing against expensive GPUs and data centers as collateral, or outside investors are purchasing assets that Big Tech then leases on a long-term basis. Nvidia is also building out a chip-based financing market, partnering with Wall Street financial firms to create large-scale AI infrastructure financing platforms.
In August, Nvidia announced plans with six financial partners—Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, and KKR—to build a platform that would attract more than $500 billion in third-party capital. Nvidia also proposed sharing up to $125 billion in risk by guaranteeing hardware residual values.
Recent major financing deals using similar structures include:
Partnership with 6 financial firms
Amazon's move is part of a broader trend of converting AI infrastructure into financial products. The structure—where physical assets like semiconductors and data centers serve as underlying assets for bond issuance—resembles past aircraft leasing or real estate securitization, but carries differentiated risk given the rapid pace of technological obsolescence in AI chips.
As Nvidia's chip generation cycles accelerate, there are concerns that the value of assets held by SPVs could decline faster than expected.
Grace Blackwell is Nvidia's cutting-edge chip, but the next-generation "Vera Rubin" is slated to succeed it. Amazon tells investors that each generation of semiconductors has a useful life of at least five years. Starting in 2025, the company also shortened the useful life of certain server and networking equipment from six years to five years. However, the structure—in which Amazon guarantees long-term use as lessee—provides investors with a degree of cash flow stability.
Market observers believe that if this deal closes, other Big Tech companies are likely to adopt similar approaches. With AI infrastructure investment now measured in the hundreds of billions of dollars, asset securitization offers a practical alternative for improving capital efficiency.
Amazon has not issued an official statement regarding the specific terms of the deal or the list of investors. However, industry observers interpret the structure as a strategic choice by Big Tech to simultaneously achieve two goals: expanding AI investment and maintaining financial soundness.

