ARTICLE Will Nvidia be allowed to sell more chips to China? This former Trump economic adviser has a hot take.
When Nvidia ( NVDA ) CEO Jensen Huang arrives at the state dinner on Thursday between President Trump and Chinese President Xi Jinping, you better believe he'll be hoping to get a sense of when — and to what extent — his business may resume in China.
Both sides coming to a deal on this front won't be easy, given the politics still at play.
"My personal opinion is that we need to have as much competitive advantage here in the United States as we can, because we've got competitive disadvantages against China," said Gary Cohn, IBM ( IBM ) vice chair and former economic adviser to President Trump in his first term, on Yahoo Finance's Sozzi Unleashed . "You know, they don't have environmental issues. They don't have a cost of capital. They can build data centers wherever they want. The citizens can't stand up and say not in my backyard."
Cohn added, "To the extent that the Chinese have all these other significant advantages, the one advantage we have is we've got the chips, and we've got the chip manufacturing here. I'd like to keep that advantage as long as we can. "
The Trump administration has allowed Nvidia to ship its H200 AI chips to approved customers in China. The problem for Nvidia: The H200 is already an older-generation product, trailing Nvidia's current Blackwell chips in terms of performance.
In other words, Nvidia is likely leaving billions in sales on the table by being unable to get Blackwell chips into China to feed the country's AI startup scene.
Chinese authorities, however, have so far restricted shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips.
While Nvidia hasn't factored any sales from China into its outlook for the year, the stock is still up 21% year to date, powered by the AI boom here in the US.

 93 TOPIC The remaining balance of Nvidia's equity-investment commitments, the cash paid to fund them, and any change in the committed amounts, as disclosed in Nvidia's next annual report. DESC How much of Nvidia's $25 billion of equity-investment commitments outstanding at July 26, 2026 will be funded or remain outstanding at its next annual report? QUESTION {'object': 'NVDA', 'kind': 'asset', 'focus': "The remaining balance of Nvidia's equity-investment commitments, the cash paid to fund them, and any change in the committed amounts, as disclosed in Nvidia's next annual report.", 'question': "What will Nvidia's remaining balance of equity-investment commitments be at the end of FY2027, as disclosed in its FY2027 Form 10-K?", 'year': 'FY2027', 'due_day': '2027-04-01', 'rungs': [{'label': 'Strongly bullish', 'condition': 'below 15,000,000,000 USD'}, {'label': 'Bullish', 'condition': 'from 15,000,000,000 USD to below 22,500,000,000 USD'}, {'label': 'Neutral', 'condition': 'from 22,500,000,000 USD to below 27,500,000,000 USD (the amount last reported when the question opened: "Nvidia also disclosed $25 billion of equity-investment commitments at July 26.")'}, {'label': 'Bearish', 'condition': 'from 27,500,000,000 USD to below 35,000,000,000 USD'}, {'label': 'Strongly bearish', 'condition': '35,000,000,000 USD or more'}]} OTHER {'not_judged': [{'part': 'How much of the commitments will be funded by Nvidia.', 'why': 'The judged figure is the remaining commitment balance, not the amount of cash paid to fund commitments.'}], 'reference': {'words': 'Nvidia also disclosed $25 billion of equity-investment commitments at July 26.', 'kind': 'last_reported', 'value': '25000000000', 'value_to': None}, 'favourable_end': 'lower', 'favourable_why': "A lower remaining balance means fewer future equity-investment commitments constrain Nvidia's flexibility.", 'ruled_out_by': None, 'due_day_basis': 'expected_publication'}
ARTICLE Nvidia passed our low-debt and strong-cash-flow screen. See the other businesses that cleared those financial hurdles alongside it . The chip business generates substantial cash, but shareholders also need to account for money invested in other companies. That spending sits outside the usual free-cash-flow calculation and can change how much remains for capital returns.
NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) reported August 26 that second-quarter revenue reached $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year. Paid demand for its computing infrastructure supports the AI investment case. The narrower question is whether cash from that business covers both ecosystem investments and shareholder distributions.
The investment bill sits below free cash flow
Over the first half of fiscal 2027, operating cash flow was $74.421 billion. Subtracting $4.434 billion of equipment and intangible-asset purchases plus $92 million of related principal payments leaves $69.895 billion of company-defined free cash flow. That is a useful cash measure, but it leaves out $42.404 billion spent purchasing equity securities. Sales of equity securities brought in $7.241 billion, making the net investment outflow $35.163 billion.
After that net investment spending, the same operating-cash base leaves $34.732 billion. Nvidia paid $39.044 billion for repurchases and $6.290 billion in dividends, a combined $45.334 billion. Net equity purchases consumed 50.3% of free cash flow. The resulting $10.602 billion gap required other funding on this narrow calculation. Debt issuance supplied $24.896 billion of cash; existing liquidity and other cash movements also matter. Our Check Point analysis examines when strong liquidity and a buyback program depend on financing beyond cash produced by the business .
This bridge includes actual cash paid, not the entire value of Nvidia's investment portfolio. It also leaves acquisitions, employee-tax payments and other uses outside the calculation. The $99 billion equity-investment balance at July 26 measures an asset position; it is not a bill payable again next quarter.
Nvidia also disclosed $25 billion of equity-investment commitments at July 26. Its August data-center guarantees carry a conditional aggregate cap of $105 billion, with exposure beginning as facilities enter service, expected from fiscal 2029. Those future and contingent amounts belong outside the current cash bridge. Our earlier portfolio analysis follows the investment risks linking Nvidia, Intel and CoreWeave .
Holding equipment spending, net equity investment and distributions constant, operating cash would need to rise 14.2% to close the identified gap. Alternatively, net equity purchases would need to fall to $24.561 billion. These are funding thresholds, not forecasts. Investment sales and repurchases are discretionary, while commitments can constrain future flexibility.
The bull case is that ecosystem investments broaden computing adoption and support future product demand. Nvidia's growing sales already provide a substantial cash engine. The bear case is that weaker customer funding or deployment delays could hurt both chip collections and the companies in its investment portfolio.
At October 9 prices, Nvidia traded at 43.6 times reported trailing free cash flow. That denominator excludes equity purchases, so it cannot also be treated as cash entirely available for distributions. Insider Monkey counted 285 Nvidia holders in Q2 2026, up from 275 in Q1; Fisher increased its shares 3%. Those positions predate the August results. The next cash statement should show whether operating collections are keeping pace with the full capital-allocation program.

 96 TOPIC Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated. DESC Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date? QUESTION {'object': 'NVDA', 'kind': 'asset', 'focus': "Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.", 'question': 'Will NVIDIA complete its acquisition of Hugging Face by 2028-03-24, as disclosed in an NVIDIA filing or completion announcement?', 'year': '2028', 'due_day': '2028-03-24', 'rungs': [{'label': 'Yes', 'condition': 'NVIDIA completes the Hugging Face acquisition by 2028-03-24, as disclosed in an NVIDIA filing or completion announcement.'}, {'label': 'Ruled out', 'condition': 'NVIDIA and Hugging Face formally terminate or abandon the acquisition agreement after 2026-09-25. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-25 and stands on 2028-03-24, and only when rung 1 does not.'}, {'label': 'Still open', 'condition': 'Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-03-24.'}]} OTHER {'not_judged': [], 'reference': None, 'favourable_end': None, 'favourable_why': None, 'ruled_out_by': 'NVIDIA and Hugging Face', 'due_day_basis': 'stated'}
ARTICLE The artificial intelligence boom has transformed the semiconductor landscape. Deciding between a high-growth connectivity specialist like Credo Technology Group ( CRDO +5.75% ) and the industry giant Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) requires a look at their specific niches.
Both companies are central to modern data centers, but they solve different problems. Credo provides the high-speed wiring and connectivity chips that keep data moving, while Nvidia supplies the massive processing power AI models require. Comparing these two reveals how different players profit from the same infrastructure trend.
Credo designs high-speed copper and optical interconnect solutions essential for modern AI-driven infrastructure. The company operates as a niche provider of high-speed connectivity solutions among semiconductor stocks serving AI data centers. Credo relies on a limited number of customers for a substantial portion of its revenue, with sales to its top 10 customers accounting for approximately 90% of total revenue in fiscal 2026. This level of customer concentration adds risk to the business. The company also maintains a partnership with Oracle ( ORCL -0.13% ) to develop ZeroFlap optics to improve reliability in AI data centers.
Financial performance has accelerated significantly recently. In fiscal 2026, which ended in May 2026, revenue reached nearly $1.3 billion, up roughly 205.7% from the previous year. This growth helped the company achieve a net income of approximately $472.3 million. The net margin for the period was approximately 35.4%, a substantial improvement from the 11.9% net margin reported in the prior fiscal year.
The balance sheet remains highly liquid. As of its May 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio (a measure of short-term debt-paying ability) is nearly 10.2x. The debt-to-equity ratio (total debt compared to equity) is 0.0x, indicating no debt relative to equity. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) was nearly $407.0 million. Stock-based compensation represented roughly 39.3% of operating cash flow, which inflates reported cash generation because SBC is a non-cash expense added back in the cash flow statement.
Nvidia is the dominant provider of GPU-accelerated computing platforms used in gaming, professional visualization, and data centers. The company sells its products to massive cloud service providers and AI model makers globally. Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively. This level of customer concentration adds a layer of risk to the business. The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.
Revenue growth remains a primary driver for the business. In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.
Strong profitability supports a robust capital structure. As of its January 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio is approximately 3.9x. The debt-to-equity ratio (which compares total debt to shareholder equity) is under 0.1x, meaning the company carries very little debt. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) reached nearly $96.7 billion for the fiscal year. This massive cash generation provides significant flexibility for research, development, and strategic acquisitions.
Credo faces intense competition from larger, well-established semiconductor companies and potential new entrants in the data infrastructure market. The company depends heavily on a small number of customers, making it vulnerable to losses or shifts in purchasing patterns. Its business model relies on third-party manufacturing partners, exposing the company to supply chain disruptions and geopolitical risks regarding operations in Taiwan and mainland China. Furthermore, evolving international trade controls and export restrictions could restrict its ability to serve key markets.
Nvidia faces significant risks from global geopolitical tensions and export control regulations, particularly for high-performance AI chips. These restrictions have impacted sales to China and could continue to limit market access. The company faces intense competition from other hardware suppliers and internal development efforts by customers like Microsoft ( MSFT +1.22% ) , Amazon ( AMZN +0.51% ) , and Alphabet ( GOOGL -0.61% ) ( GOOG -0.77% ) . Nvidia also manages various legal challenges, including inquiries from competition regulators worldwide regarding its market practices.
Nvidia trades at a lower forward P/E based on future earnings estimates, while both carry a high P/S ratio compared to historical averages.
I'd buy Nvidia, and it isn't close. Here's why:
Credo's growth is not the issue. Revenue in its latest quarter more than doubled from a year earlier, and management guided the current quarter higher still. The stock fell anyway because gross margin guidance came in flat, and shares now sit well below their 52-week high. That's the problem with a company that gets about 90% of its revenue from 10 customers. Any hint of slower spending from one of them moves the stock, and the valuation leaves no room for a miss.
Nvidia is growing nearly as fast at a scale that makes Credo look like a rounding error. Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all. Nvidia has its own concentration risk, with two customers making up more than a third of sales, and the Hugging Face deal shows it's willing to spend to stay ahead.
For a long-term investor, Nvidia offers the same AI infrastructure exposure at a lower price per dollar of expected earnings, with far less dependence on any single buyer. Credo belongs in a portfolio only as a small position you can afford to watch swing.
Credo more than tripled revenue last year but leans on 10 customers for 90% of sales, while Nvidia is doubling revenue at a scale nobody else in AI hardware can match.
By Mike Schwenk – Updated Sep 25, 2026 at 3:00PM EST
Credo makes the high-speed connectivity chips and cables that move data between servers inside AI data centers, and revenue more than tripled in fiscal 2026.
Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.
Both companies depend on a small group of customers, but Nvidia's scale, margins, and lower valuation make it the stronger buy today.

